Describe Microsoft Dataverse

Date: September 17, 2026
Platform / Environment: Microsoft Learn — Microsoft Power Platform

What I Did

Completed the Microsoft Learn module Describe Microsoft Dataverse.

The module covered:

* Differences between traditional databases and Microsoft Dataverse
* Dataverse tables, columns and relationships
* Business logic options in Dataverse
* Table forms and views
* Creating and editing tables, columns and relationships through conversations
* Power Fx
* How Dataverse supports Power Platform and AI/Copilot experiences

What I Learned

I learned that Microsoft Dataverse is more than simply a place to store data. It provides a structured data foundation for Power Platform applications and can support solutions that integrate data, business logic, security and AI capabilities.

I also learned how Dataverse organises information using tables, columns and relationships, and how forms and views provide different ways of working with that data.

The module also introduced using natural-language conversations to create and modify Dataverse components, which connects Dataverse with Microsoft’s increasing use of AI-assisted development.

Key Takeaway

Dataverse provides a managed, structured data layer that Power Platform solutions can build on, rather than requiring someone to build and maintain a traditional database from scratch.

Understanding the relationship between data → business logic → applications → AI is an important part of understanding the wider Power Platform ecosystem.

Practical Relevance

For IT and cloud support, this gives me a better foundation for understanding Microsoft environments where organisations use Power Apps, Power Automate, Copilot and other Power Platform services.

Next Step

Continue learning how Dataverse connects with Power Apps, Power Automate and Copilot, then get some practical experience working with tables and relationships.
